UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I understand the club lead and it could be right. But in general you try  avoiding leading suits the opponents have bid. Admittedly I might only have 3 clubs, but that is unlikely. Given I bid 1@C, that is likely to give away a trick, and it did as you saw at trick 6.|
PG| ***** 17 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That would have been a great Match Point score as it is easy to hold me to 8 tricks at most and perhaps even 7. But defense is hard, although most of the defense plays were reasonable (although I do question the club leads). Leading the club and ducking the diamond both turned out to cost tricks.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BEast: ^*NCan West make a ^*BDelayed DONT^*n bid after 1NT by South?|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|There is a convention called ^*BDelayed DONT^*n that is rarely played but I like it.|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Normally |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=dont,DONT^*N|CP||FP||at| only applies after a 1NT opener. But if you play Delayed DONT, then you can bid DONT after any 1N bid, be it opener, response or rebid. So in this hand, if you play that, then West could bid 2@C over 1N, to shows 5-4 in clubs and another suit.

|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


